# Platform-capability gaps: functionality `core` (and, eventually, the rest
# of `std`) needs a concrete answer for, but has no single portable one --
# the target platform, or the final application, supplies exactly one
# implementation via a `glue` block. See docs/21-gaps-and-glue.md for the
# full design; this file only ever adds new gaps, never their glue.

import option::Option;

# The global heap allocator. Every function is required (no default body --
# see docs/21-gaps-and-glue.md for why a default-bodied gap function isn't
# supported yet) and self-less: called through the gap's own qualified name
# (`core::platform::GlobalAllocator::alloc(...)`), never through an instance.
#
# `alloc`/`realloc` return `*mut u8`, not `*u8` -- freshly allocated memory
# is always meant to be written into, and a plain `*u8` can never be cast to
# a `*mut T` (mutable-target-needs-mutable-source, the same directional rule
# ordinary pointer coercion enforces), which would make writing into a new
# allocation impossible for any caller. `free`'s own parameter stays a plain
# `*u8` -- freeing needs an address, not write access, and a `*mut u8`
# argument widens to it for free either way.
gap GlobalAllocator {
    alloc(size: usize) => *mut u8;
    free(ptr: *u8) => void;
    realloc(ptr: *u8, size: usize) => *mut u8;
}

# Console capabilities stay separate: a platform can provide output without
# input. `None` is failure; `Some(0)` remains a valid short write or EOF.
gap StandardOutput {
    write(bytes: *[]u8) => Option<usize>;
}

gap StandardError {
    write(bytes: *[]u8) => Option<usize>;
}

gap StandardInput {
    read(into: *mut []u8) => Option<usize>;
}
